TOOL_RESULT retrieve_history:
{"results": [], "n_results": 0}

Stage 2. Using the retrieval result above (follow-up tools are allowed within the tool budget), complete steps 4 to 9
and reply with the final action. Output schema (all fields required):
{
 "research_concepts": [
  "concept"
 ],
 "methodological_questions": [
  "question"
 ],
 "historical_evidence": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a record returned by a tool",
   "status_as_recorded": "outcome status exactly as stated in the record (supported | rejected | inconclusive | untestable | artifact | not a hypothesis test | no result recorded)",
   "finding": "what the record states, in its own terms"
  }
 ],
 "failed_approaches": [
  {
   "id": "identifier of a returned record",
   "what_failed": "approach",
   "why": "failure mechanism or limitation as recorded"
  }
 ],
 "evidence_establishes": [
  "what the retrieved evidence establishes for this question"
 ],
 "evidence_does_not_establish": [
  "what it leaves open or cannot show"
 ],
 "own_inferences": [
  "your inference, not stated in any record"
 ],
 "uncertainties": [
  "uncertainty or missing information"
 ],
 "historical_effect": {
  "changed_decision": "yes | no | no_historical_evidence",
  "explanation": "whether and how the historical evidence changed the decision"
 },
 "testability": {
  "p_testable": "number in [0,1]: probability the question can be answered with the stated data at useful power",
  "blocking_constraints": [
   {
    "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
    "text": "string"
   }
  ]
 },
 "prior_work": {
  "already_answered": "yes | partly | no | unknown",
  "cited": [
   "identifiers of prior work relied on"
  ],
  "summary": "string"
 },
 "risks": [
  {
   "code": "one of RISK_CODES",
   "text": "string"
  }
 ],
 "predicted_outcome": {
  "supported": "probability",
  "rejected": "probability",
  "untestable": "probability",
  "artifact": "probability",
  "uncertain": "probability"
 },
 "decision": "proceed | proceed_with_changes | do_not_proceed",
 "design": {
  "estimand": "string",
  "unit": "string",
  "sample": "string",
  "power": "string",
  "controls": "string",
  "falsification": "string",
  "multiplicity": "string",
  "decision_rule": "string",
  "data_version": "string"
 }
}
Field guidance:
- historical_evidence and failed_approaches: only records returned by a tool, with the status as recorded; empty lists
  if nothing relevant was returned.
- testability.p_testable: probability that the question can be answered with the stated data at useful statistical power.
- prior_work.already_answered: whether retrieved earlier work already answered substantially the same question.
  Use "unknown" if no earlier work was retrieved. List identifiers you relied on in prior_work.cited.
- predicted_outcome: probabilities (summing to 1) of what a competent test of the question as posed would conclude:
  supported, rejected (adequately powered null), untestable (cannot be tested with this data), artifact (apparent
  effect produced by a flaw), uncertain (inconclusive or inadequately tested).
- decision: proceed, proceed_with_changes (state the changes in design), or do_not_proceed.
- design: a pre-registration-quality plan even if the decision is do_not_proceed (then describe the minimum viable test).

{"action": "final", "output": {
  "research_concepts": [
    "player absence",
    "offensive reorganization",
    "initiation profile similarity",
    "play-type usage redistribution",
    "teammate replacement gain"
  ],
  "methodological_questions": [
    "How to define the absent player's primary play types?",
    "How to measure closest pre-absence initiation profile among teammates?",
    "How to attribute gain in specific play types to the most similar teammate?",
    "How to address confounding from simultaneous lineup changes or multiple absences?",
    "What is the statistical power given 11 cases?"
  ],
  "historical_evidence": [],
  "failed_approaches": [],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "No historical evidence was available; the retrieval returned no earlier study-family records."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Nothing is established by prior work for this specific question."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "The question posits a within-team replacement mechanism: the teammate most similar to the absent player in pre-absence play-type initiations will absorb the largest share of the absent player's primary play-type initiations.",
    "The available data records only 11 cases, likely referring to high-usage absence spells that meet strict criteria; even the 148 total spells likely include many low-usage or confounded absences, making a test of the similarity-gain association severely underpowered.",
    "The concept of 'closest initiation profile' requires arbitrary choices (which play types, how to measure similarity across different base rates, whether to use raw initiations or shares). These choices could lead to multiple testing and overfitting if not pre-registered.",
    "Observed gain in a teammate's initiations during an absence spell could arise from natural game-to-game fluctuation, opponent adjustments, or other personnel changes, making attribution to similarity difficult without strong controls.",
    "Without prior work establishing a measurement protocol or an expected effect size, a confirmatory test with 11 cases is unlikely to yield a reliable result."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "What exactly the 11 cases represent (e.g., isolated absences of high-structural-influence players with adequate pre-absence data).",
    "Whether primary play types should be defined by share of the absent player's initiations, absolute volume, or another rule.",
    "How similarity is computed across play types that vary in frequency (e.g., cosine vs. Euclidean vs. rank-based metrics).",
    "The magnitude of any true effect and whether it exceeds the background noise in small samples.",
    "How to handle teammates who already share the primary play types (ceiling effects)."
  ],
  "historical_effect": {
    "changed_decision": "no_historical_evidence",
    "explanation": "No prior records were returned, so the decision is based solely on the assessment of the task statement and data catalog."
  },
  "testability": {
    "p_testable": 0.1,
    "blocking_constraints": [
      {
        "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
        "text": "Only 11 qualifying cases (and even 148 total spells) provide extremely low power to detect a plausible effect of initiation-profile similarity on play-type gain."
      },
      {
        "code": "CONFOUNDING",
        "text": "Simultaneous lineup changes, opponent quality, game location, rest days, and other injuries are likely confounders that cannot be adequately controlled with such a small sample."
      },
      {
        "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
        "text": "Primary play-type definitions and similarity metrics are not yet validated; small pre-absence samples for some players add noise to the similarity measure."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "unknown",
    "cited": [],
    "summary": "No earlier study-family records were returned by the retrieval call."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "SAMPLE_SIZE_POWER",
      "text": "The 11 cases (and only 148 total spells) likely fail to provide enough independent variation in pre-absence similarity to power a test of the hypothesised effect."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "For many absence spells, the absent player's primary play types may be highly concentrated in a single teammate, or no teammate may be notably similar, reducing informative variation."
    },
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Play-type classification (the inferrer) and similarity measurement require operational definitions that are not yet established, introducing potential misclassification."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Lineup composition, pace, opponent defensive strength, and game context all shift during an absence spell, making it difficult to isolate the role of initiation-profile similarity."
    },
    {
      "code": "MULTIPLE_TESTING",
      "text": "The question mentions three alternative similarity criteria (profile, minutes, initiations, same-position minutes); testing all without adjustment inflates false-positive risk."
    },
    {
      "code": "MECHANICAL_FLOOR",
      "text": "Teammates who are already high-usage initiators in the absent player's primary types may have limited room to increase further, creating ceiling effects."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Over a long absence, team tactics and roles may evolve, so a single pre-absence similarity measure may not capture the dynamic redistribution of play types."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.05,
    "rejected": 0.05,
    "untestable": 0.65,
    "artifact": 0.15,
    "uncertain": 0.10
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "For each eligible absence spell, the linear association between a teammate's pre-absence initiation-profile similarity to the absent player and the subsequent change in that teammate's initiation volume in the absent player's primary play types, controlling for shared on-court minutes and positional overlap.",
    "unit": "One absence spell (a single absent player missing ≥8 consecutive games, with no concurrent injury to other rotation regulars that alters the team's structure).",
    "sample": "All such absence spells from the 2015-16 through 2025-26 NBA seasons where the absent player had ≥50 pre-absence initiations in the spell's lookback window and at least three teammates had ≥30 pre-absence initiations. This is expected to yield >> 11 cases.",
    "power": "Power to be determined via simulation on pre-2025 data; minimum target 80% power to detect a standardized regression coefficient of 0.3 at α = 0.05 (two-sided), requiring an estimated 100+ independent spells. The current 11 cases are far below this threshold.",
    "controls": "Opponent pre-game defensive rating, game location (home/away), team rest days, indicators for concurrent minor injuries, and pre-absence lineup role of the teammate (usage percentile and primary position).",
    "falsification": "Sham-absence placebo: apply the same algorithm to stretches where the focal player did not miss games (randomly chosen same-length windows from a different season segment) and verify the similarity-gain association disappears.",
    "multiplicity": "Pre-specify a single similarity metric (cosine similarity over eight play types) and the sum of initiations in the absent player's top two primary play types as the primary outcome. Secondary metrics (shared minutes, same-position minutes) reported with Bonferroni correction.",
    "decision_rule": "Support the hypothesis if the similarity coefficient is positive with p < 0.05 (one-tailed, pre-registered) and the placebo test shows no effect (p > 0.10). Reject if the 95% CI of the coefficient is bounded away from a meaningful effect size (Cohen's d < 0.1). Inconclusive otherwise.",
    "data_version": "NBA stats API play-by-play and project-derived play-type initiation records frozen as of 2026-11-01, with a fixed, versioned classifier."
  }
}}

